JetOS benchmarks
================

Benchmarks for measuring JetOS: execution times of classic real-time kernels,
and interference between partitions on shared hardware (caches, TLB, memory,
pipeline). Every benchmark builds and runs on x86_64 (board x86_64-qemu) and
arm64 (board arm64-qemu). Each benchmark directory has a README describing
what it tests and what it does.

Sets
----

A653_Benchmarks/   WCET kernels (SNU-RT, Malardalen, MiBench) as periodic
                   ARINC 653 processes: BinSearch, Compress, Fibonacci, FIR,
                   JFDCTint, LMS, LUDCMP, MIB_Basicmath, QSort, QuadRoot.
CACHE_FRAMEWORK/   Kernels for cache studies: copies of the A653 kernels plus
                   matmult, matconv, transitive and the round_array tests.
HW_Specific/       Stress tests for one hardware resource each: CACHES,
                   MEMORY, PIPELINE, TLB.
CPU_MEM_2PART/     CPU and memory stress in two identical partitions.
MEM_SORT_UNMIXED_MONO/
                   Memory-intensive generation and sorting, three partitions.
NAV_SOB_SYS/, NAV_SYS_MONO_QUEUING/, NAV_SYS_MONO_SAMPLING/
                   A navigation system over three communicating partitions
                   (sampling or queuing ports).
BENCH_TOOLS/       Shared configuration and code generators:
                   arch_config.h      cache and TLB geometry per architecture
                                      (Intel i5-750 for x86_64, Cortex-A53
                                      for arm64); adjust it for your hardware
                   config.h           settings for HW_Specific
                   gen_icache_flooder.c, gen_itlb_flooder.c
                                      generate instruction-cache and
                                      instruction-TLB flooding code

QEMU (TCG) does not model caches or TLBs: the HW_Specific results are only
meaningful on real hardware, with arch_config.h set for that processor.

Build and run
-------------

From the top-level directory:

    make benchmarks                     # all benchmarks for x86_64
    make BSP=arm64-qemu benchmarks      # all benchmarks for arm64
    make clean-benchmarks

Then run one image, for example:

    qemu-system-x86_64 -vga none -m 1G -nographic \
        -kernel benchmark/A653_Benchmarks/QSort/build/x86_64-qemu/pok.elf
    qemu-system-aarch64 -M virt -cpu cortex-a53 -m 1G -nographic \
        -kernel benchmark/A653_Benchmarks/QSort/build/arm64-qemu/pok.elf

Benchmarks run forever; quit QEMU with Ctrl-A X.
